 {"status": "complete", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-13", "title": "[dogfood:P2] allow-cat-safe's negative lookahead can never match its advertised exclusion — the rule is a silent no-op exactly where its description says it matters", "detail": "Shipped rule (plugin/terminal_jail/rules/00-builtins.yaml, allow-cat-safe, description 'Safe file reads (non-sensitive paths)'): pattern `^cat\\s(?!.*/(etc|boot|proc|sys))`. The `.*` inside the lookahead can match the empty string, so ANY command whose path starts with /etc, /boot, /proc or /sys fails the lookahead and the rule does not match — proved standalone: re.match(pattern, 'cat /etc/passwd') -> False, while 'cat /tmp/x' -> True. Consequence: on sensitive paths the rule silently vanishes and `cat /etc/passwd` is allowed by DEFAULT-ALLOW (rule_id null, live-probed) rather than by any explicit decision; on benign paths it only adds a redundant allow. The description promises discrimination the regex cannot deliver.", "priority": "P2", "source": "dogfood-cron", "reasoning": {"note": "dogfood cycle 2026-09-17 — verdict PROMISING-BUT-ROUGH (t2fs <5s, friction 5; live board re-verification pass)"}, "review_notes": "Fix: anchor the lookahead to the token boundary, e.g. `^cat\\s+(?!/(etc|boot|proc|sys)(/|\\s|$))`, and add a regression asserting the engine's matched rule for `cat /etc/passwd` is NOT allow-cat-safe (post DF-12 it should also carry a verdict reason). Keep behaviour allow (default-allow posture) unless the rule-pack work (TJ-GAP-061) introduces a read-protect pack.", "ts": "2026-09-17T10:19:41.000Z", "worker_status": "premise-false", "updated_at": "2026-09-18 03:55:00", "completed_at": "2026-09-18 03:55:00", "foreman_note": "PREMISE-FALSE 2026-09-18 (stand-in PM cycle, live-verified, no code change): the load-bearing claim does not hold. Bridge probes re-run by the PM: \"cat /etc/passwd\" -> action=allow rule_id=null (the negative lookahead DOES exclude the advertised prefixes, so the rule does not mis-attribute them) and \"cat /tmp/x\" -> action=allow rule_id=allow-cat-safe; specs/interruptor.md:169-172 documents the deliberate non-match plus default-allow as intended, not a bug. The row proposed anchoring the lookahead to a token boundary; diffed against the shipped pattern over 7 vectors the anchored form changes ONLY attribution, and only for paths that mention /etc later in the string (\"cat /etcfoo\", \"cat /tmp/notes/etc/passwd\", \"cat  /etc/passwd\", \"cat /tmp/x /etc/y\") - every one of those stays action=allow either way, so the proposed fix cannot change a single verdict. Residual (cosmetic, no code change): the rule description \"Safe file reads (non-sensitive paths)\" reads as a discrimination promise; the rule catalog work in DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-9 is the right home for that wording. Shipped pattern left byte-identical."}
 {"status": "complete", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-14", "title": "[dogfood:P2] SKIPPED-install-bunker (2026-09-17 run): bunker spawn broken on las-bunker-03 — deadline_exceeded x2, half-spawned users get no rootless docker", "detail": "This run could NOT prove fresh-machine installability. Evidence: `bunker spawn --server bunker-las-03 --ttl 2h` failed twice with 'bunker: spawn agent: deadline_exceeded: context deadline exceeded' (CLI 0.1.3, has the 300s spawn fix); `bunker list --server bunker-las-03` -> 'No agents found.'; over root ssh the box shows the half-spawned users my attempts created (bunker-0d376e5f home dir 03:09, bunker-cefbb3c6 03:11 PDT) with NO /run/user/<uid>/docker.sock for ANY bunker user — the fresh-agent rootless-docker install never completes. Host otherwise healthy: ssh ok, `systemctl is-active bunkerd` = active, docker 26.1.5, outbound net 200 (get.docker.com). Regression vs the 2026-09-15 run where the bunker leg PASSED (clone->install 0s->smoke ok). Cleanup done by this run: both of my half-spawned users removed via userdel (one needed kill of pid 77362 first); 3 PRE-EXISTING stale users left untouched (bunker-988dd3f7, bunker-5a9ce50d, bunker-cae7195b).", "priority": "P2", "source": "dogfood-cron", "reasoning": {"note": "dogfood cycle 2026-09-17 — verdict PROMISING-BUT-ROUGH (t2fs <5s, friction 5; live board re-verification pass)"}, "review_notes": "Action (infra owner, not foreman): diagnose bunkerd's rootless-docker install path on las-bunker-03 (agent-side install step timing out under the CLI deadline — check bunkerd logs ~03:09-03:11 PDT). Re-run the bunker install leg on the next dogfood tick; until then installability evidence = local scratch-prefix install (passed this run: TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR install in 1s, live ~/.config rules", "ts": "2026-09-17T10:19:41.000Z", "worker_status": "premise-false", "updated_at": "2026-09-18 03:55:00", "completed_at": "2026-09-18 03:55:00", "foreman_note": "PREMISE-FALSE 2026-09-18 (stand-in PM cycle, live-verified on the host, no code change): the blanket host claim is refuted. PM re-probe over root ssh to bunker-las-03, per-user /run/user/<uid>/docker.sock check -> bunker-16fcd90e HAS_SOCK, bunker-5a9ce50d HAS_SOCK, bunker-c48ffb8c HAS_SOCK, bunker-cae7195b HAS_SOCK (the user the row itself dated inside its own failure window), bunker-d56a41f1 HAS_SOCK, bunker-e84df069 HAS_SOCK, bunker-988dd3f7 NO_SOCK, bunker-b5be08c6 NO_SOCK, bunker-b97e8e8c NO_SOCK; systemctl is-active bunkerd -> active. So 6 of 9 bunker users DO have the rootless docker socket the row says no user has. The residual class (a transient \"bunker spawn ... deadline_exceeded\" on that host) is host-side: owner = the bunker host / the dogfood install leg, and no terminal-jail code change can affect it. Retired as a consumer-board harness artifact per the PM dedupe pass; re-file against the host if it recurs."}
